| `link` inside a handler: the path is a value, and the name it binds is an
| ordinary variable holding an address rather than a compile-time alias. This
| is what lets a program open an organelle it only learned about while
| running — see `ast::Stmt::LinkRuntime`.

| A note on the paths below. A top-level `link` is resolved by `loader.rs`
| against the *file* that says it, which is why other fixtures write
| `native_modules/x.so`. A `link` inside a handler cannot work that way: its
| path is a value that does not exist until the program runs, and a compiled
| binary carries no source tree to resolve against. So it is taken as an
| ordinary path, relative to the working directory — and these fixtures run
| from the crate root.
let held = null

Open { path } => {
    link path as m
    emit Double { value = 21 } to m get r
    unlink m
    return r
}

emit Open { path = "tests/native_modules/test_math.so" } to this get answer
assert answer.value = 42

| Again, after the first was unlinked: opening is not a one-time act, and
| nothing about the first link is baked into the program.
emit Open { path = "tests/native_modules/test_math.so" } to this get again
assert again.value = 42

| An address outlives the handler that made it — it is an ordinary value the
| program can keep, which is the whole reason it is a value and not a name.
Keep { path } => {
    link path as m
    held = m
    return Kept {}
}

emit Keep { path = "tests/native_modules/test_math.so" } to this get k
assert k._class = "Kept"
emit Double { value = 5 } to held get later
assert later.value = 10

| The same file linked twice is **two organelles**, not two names for one. A
| module has state, so a second link is a second thing — different address,
| and stopping one leaves the other running.
SecondName { path } => {
    link path as m
    return Named { address = m }
}

emit SecondName { path = "tests/native_modules/test_math.so" } to this get second
assert not second.address = held

unlink second.address
emit Double { value = 6 } to held get survives
assert survives.value = 12

| And now stopping it. Asked from inside a handler,
| because at the top level a failure ends the program rather than becoming a
| value — the address is stale either way, this is just where it can be
| looked at.
Stale {} => {
    emit Double { value = 7 } to held get gone
    return gone
}

unlink held
emit Stale {} to this get gone
assert gone._class = "Exception"

| An organelle that speaks first can be linked here too, and is heard: its
| queue joins the same list a top-level `link` adds to, so the program's own
| drain empties it and each answer goes back to the module that pushed.
|
| This is what lets an application choose its door while running — it cannot
| know at build time whether it is opening a port of its own or being handed
| one by a host.
Tick { value } => {
    return Ack { value = value + 100 }
}

Speaking {} => {
    link "tests/native_modules/test_events.so" as ev
    emit Start { value = 3 } to ev get started
    assert started.value = 3
    return Opened { door = ev }
}

emit Speaking {} to this get spoke
assert spoke._class = "Opened"
let door = spoke.door

| The three ticks have been handled by here, and each answer went back —
| through a queue that did not exist when this program started.
emit Answers {} to door get a
assert a.count = 3
assert a.last = 102

| And it can be stopped again — which is only true because it stopped
| speaking first: `unlink` refuses while anything an organelle holds is
| still working.
unlink door
